00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:20.689 align:start position:0%
 
ah  it's my privilege to welcome dr. Daniel  B Copeland who's an associate professor  of education at Hillsdale College he  received his PhD in education from  Michigan State University and at  Hillsdale he serves as an advisor for  the classical education minor and the  liberal arts teacher apprenticeship in  addition to serving as a consultant for  the college's charter school initiative  he also serves on the board of directors  of the Society for classical learning  and on the editorial board for arts of  Liberty but journal on liberal arts and  liberal education in 2013 dr. Copeland  was voted professor of the Year by  Hillsdale senior class please welcome me  in joining dr. Daniel Copeland  well thank you for the great  introduction I'm really pleased to be  here and I'm gonna spend a little bit of  time talking about common core and I'd  be happy to answer your questions at the  end on May 29th 2009 Arne Duncan the new  Secretary of Education for the old of  the Obama administration gave a speech  at the National Press Club here in  Washington DC in the speech he said  quote we want to raise the bar  dramatically in terms of high standards  what we have as a country I'm convinced  is what we call a race to the bottom we  have 50 different standards 50 different  goal posts and dual and due to political  pressure those have all been dumbed down  we want to fundamentally reverse that we  want common career-ready internationally  benchmarked standards in this short  paragraph the Secretary of Education  identified the problems of the past and  set a new vision for education in this  country he correctly assessed the the  damage created in the Bush  administration's education policy from  2002 known as No Child Left Behind or  NCLB while supporters of NCLB can point  to limited success in a few areas of the  Bush administration's education policy  left the nation schools a bureaucratic  mess in the National Press Club speech  the new Secretary of Education was  arguing that the mess was created by  what he and others have called a  patchwork of state standards that left  the states to compete in a fundamentally  flawed and unfair process for limited  federal funds secretary Duncan's  argument presented at the National Press  Club and elsewhere was very persuasive  to those in the education community who  had suffered under the separate but very  unequal policy  of the era known as No Child Left Behind  four years later after the 2009 speech  all but a handful of states have signed  on to a common set of curriculum  standards known as Common Core Common  Core will now provide the framework for  what students will learn in math and  English language arts but it'll also  establish two federally funded and  approved tests that will replace what  states currently use to measure their  students academic success afraid to be  left out of the new education  marketplace private companies are  quickly trying to align themselves with  Common Core Standards in order to  survive in the Common Core era the  textbook publishers and other related  industries must show how their materials  align with common core SAT and a CT  tests are now lined with Common Core as  well and those who think they can avoid  Common Core but by having their  children's attend private schools or by  homeschooling should think again the  Iowa test of the basic skills and stand  for 10 - very popular tests with private  schools and with home schoolers will  also be aligned to common core within a  few short years Common Core has gone  from virtual unknown to national  education powerhouse that may influence  the formal education of some 50 million  k-12 students in America in the next few  minutes I'll try to give you some  insight on what Common Core is what the  major arguments are both for and against  and I'll also try to show how these  arguments are missing the most important  ideas about education altogether but  first I'll start with a brief history  the idea of a rich educational  experience has its roots deep in  American history of the founders of this  country believes and informs  citizenry was necessary for good  government in the 1800s  Horace Mann continued this legacy by  arguing for widespread public education  in the late 18-hundreds policy  politicians and social leaders look to  the schools to solve pressing social  needs brought on by industrialization  urbanization and immigration many many  leading educators that leading education  theorists of the late 19th and early  20th century including John Dewey  William H Kilpatrick G Stanley Hall and  others developed and promoted  progressive solutions to these pressing  social needs for the first half of the  20th century progressive theories such  as child centered teaching and practical  work-related curricula pretty much  dominated the education landscape in  October 1957 I'm sorry the United States  was awakened from its educational  malaise when the Soviet Union  successively launched Sputnik the first  space satellite into orbit this one  event signaled America's educational  decline and brought attention to the  need for a return to rich content at  least in the fields of math science and  foreign languages but these reforms were  quickly lost in the cultural turmoil of  1960s and 70s and schools once again  offered a smorgasbord of academically  weak classes students were earning  academic credit in courses titled  personal relationships what's happening  and Girl Talk and they were receiving  academic credit for extracurricular  activities in student government mass  media and cheerleading  in 1983 the National Commission on  excellence in education published a  landmark study on American education  titled a nation at risk which warned  that our country's economic cultural and  political future was threatened by our  weak education system the report stated  the now-famous lines  quote our nation is at risk the  educational foundations of society are  presently being eroded by the rising  tide of mediocrity that threatens our  very future as a nation and a people if  an unfriendly power had attempted to  impose on America the mediocre education  performance that exists today we might  well have viewed it as an act of war end  quote  a nation at risk signaled a turning  point in American education and brought  about renewed concentration on what  America's children should know and be  able to do Eadie Hirsch's 1987 book  cultural literacy argued that schools  should concentrate on the basics and  pass along core knowledge that every  educated American should know but many  in the education establishment resisted  these content-based reforms and continue  to push a progressive agenda in  America's schools with the collapse of  the Soviet Union in the 1980s in the end  of the Cold War many countries had  greater opportunities to participate in  the global marketplace globalization led  to international comparisons across a  variety of indicators including in  education many Asian countries with whom  we were now competing seemed to be  moving further and further ahead of the  United States one of the obvious  features of the education in these  countries was the existence of clear  national standards many reformers pushed  the idea in the United States or that if  the United States was  going to compete in the international  marketplace the quality of education in  the entire country would have to improve  they also concluded that such  improvement would only occur if students  were held to high academic standards in  1989 President George W I'm sorry  President George Bush the senior hosted  an educational conference in the nation  I'm sorry  conference for the nation's governors on  academic standards and testing Acuras  Matic Governor from Arkansas named  William Jefferson Clinton took the lead  in crafting a set of goals for  increasing academic achievement in  America and when Clinton defeated Bush  for the presidency three years later the  new President used these goals to craft  his signature education policy known as  goals 2000 goals 2000 provided money for  each state to develop its own standards  that were based on a national template  critics of the initiative claimed that  this effort violated the long-standing  principle established by the Tenth  Amendment of the US Constitution that  education was the responsibility of the  states but the Clinton administration  countered that the national standards  were met only to be a template for the  standards are sorry for the states to  follow and each state was ultimately  responsible for its own standards  interestingly goals 2000 also authorized  the creation of an approval board that  would certify state standards that  they'd had actually matched the National  template the approval board however  never materialized because in the 1994  midterm election the Republicans gained  the majority in Congress and they  quickly  I'm abolished the entire  bored even without the federal board the  effort to create state standards that  were based on a national template  continued in the mid 1990s professional  subject specific organizations release  national standards in history English  and math the general public assumed that  these standards would represent the  basic knowledge and skills that students  would need in these specific areas but  they soon discovered that these  professional organizations had used this  project to push unproven and perhaps  radical ideas within the academic fields  public opposition to these national  standards spread quickly  most states avoided the controversy of  the national standards by creating their  own unique standards if there was one  thing in common across these state  standards it was there are their  emphasis on less controversial skills  such as critical thinking cooperative  learning and shared understanding rather  than concrete statements about specific  ideas people and the books that students  should read in 2001 President George  Bush jr. pushed his education policy  known as No Child Left Behind or NCLB  which like those before it promised to  increase student achievement by  encouraging States to set high standards  and develop tests that were based on  those standards but unlike the in but  unlike the initiatives that had come  before it  NCLB required States to test all  students in particular subjects at  particular grade levels in order to  receive money looking back most  education experts both on the right and  the left concluded that NCLB had failed  to deliver real and lasting success  NCLB created an environment where  teaching to the test became the status  quo and what made matters worse was that  from state to stay  the tests were all different under NCLB  each statement had it each state had its  own academic standards that it was  expected to meet and because other money  was up based on each state meeting its  own standards  there was little motivation for states  to keep the academic standards high in  an effort to show higher proficiency and  student achievement students but I'm  sorry states begin lowering proficiency  levels in what the Secretary of  Education referred to as a race to the  bottom and by the end of the decade many  in the education community were looking  for alternatives to the separate and  unequal approach to standards from NCLB  in 2007 to trade organizations than  National Governors Association in the  Council of Chief state school officers  began work on a common set of curricular  standards in English language arts and  math in December of 2008 these two  groups produced I'm a document on  national standards that would guide the  new administration during its transition  into office two months later how the  Secretary of Education announced a new  grant program known as race to the top  the name was an obvious nod to the  failures of No Child Left Behind the  program included money from the 2009  stimulus bill which was used by the  states to improve academic standards and  tests in order to receive the race to  the top grant states had to commit to  quote a set of content standards that  define what students must know and be  able to do that are substantially  identical across all states in a  consortium end quote in 2011  I the obama administration made the  decision to adopt common standards even  easier  most states were still obligated to meet  the onerous NCLB requirements the US  Department of Education promised NCLB  waivers to states that adopted common  set of college and career-ready  standards and assessments and while the  US Department of Education did not  require States to adopt the common core  standards these standards were and still  are the only standards that met the  Education Department's criteria 45  states and the District of Columbia I'm  adopted the common core standards  Minnesota adopted the English language  arts standards but it rejected the math  standards initially only Alaska and  Texas rejected common core but in the  end of Virginia and Nebraska did to the  idea of common academic standards across  all states is quite appealing to many in  the field of education because it seems  secure some obvious and long-standing  problems allow me to highlight two of  the most important first and our mobile  society it makes it very easy for people  to pick up and move as Eadie Hirsch  points out quote in a typical American  School District the average rate at  which students transfer in and out of  schools during an academic year is about  one third in a typical urban school only  about half of the students who start in  September are still there in May when  students move from school to school  especially when these moves are across  state lines they often experience a  fractured education filled with huge  gaps and boring or boring repetitions  however if all schools are meeting the  same academic standards the students  have a greater chance at a relatively  assist in education regardless of where  they move within the country in theory  students should be able to move from  Maine to California with very little  interruption in their education number  two for years the United States has  lagged behind many of the industrialized  countries in key academic areas such as  math and science since Sputnik  policymakers have tried to craft a  coherent plan to improve our country  standing in the world but they have  struggled to do this in light of the  patchwork of state standards pointing to  the failures of NCLB proponents of  Common Core argue that having a common  set of academically rigorous standards  for the entire country would allow other  policymakers to craft a coherent plan  for improving American education many  corporate leaders and policymakers argue  that if we are if we are unable to  compete as an as a nation in a global  sea I'm sorry many corporate leaders and  politicians argue that we are unable to  compete as a nation in a global society  if every state is doing its own thing  the arguments against common core as you  can probably guess Common Core has its  critics who typically concentrate on one  or more of the following concerns number  one cost critics claim that Common Core  will be very expensive to implement and  maintain the only study on the cost of  implementing Common Core and its tests  on across other nation estimate the  price tag of sixteen billion dollars  over seven years but the truth of the  matter is that no one really knows what  the final price tag will be for this  reason and others critics have already  labeled this program Obama core critics  of Common  charge that most states acted  irresponsibly when they when they chose  to adopt other standards because they  did not have a firm understanding of the  price tag  many states saw the race to the top  month  I'm sorry money as a way to pay for  immediate education expenses but they  failed to see that they were signing on  for something far worse  number two quality critics argue that  rather than pushing states towards high  standards Common Core is encouraging a  coalescence at the mediocre middle  for example while Mississippi standards  appear to be getting stronger  Massachusetts by the standards in  Massachusetts get weaker a few curricula  experts including Zev Worman Sandra's  statsky James Milgram had examined the  math and English language arts standards  very carefully and they discovered some  alarming concerns in fact because of  these concerns and others Starsky and  Milgram who served on the common core's  validation committee refused to sign the  validation report number three privacy  the 2009 stimulus bill required States  to begin tracking students in a database  starting with their preschool years -  the entry into the workforce the  database will link students results on  common core related tests to other  private personal information the  database will be available to a wide  variety of people within the government  while supporters of common core claim  that the system employs a variety of  measures to protect the anonymity of  students critics have pointed to studies  that demonstrate how these measures may  not be as secure as supporters assume  however the larger issue remains about  whether collect  this kind of information is consistent  with the role of government that we have  in this country  number four constitutionality of the  biggest concern of common core critics  to date has been the ever creasing in  role of the national government in  education the Tenth Amendment of the US  Constitution established the the  principle that the power to oversee  education belongs to the states this  long-standing principle of local control  of Education is reiterated throughout  our laws and our government codes for  generations Americans have understood  that the constitutional authority for  education rests with the states or local  government not the federal government  critics of common core see that these  standards are federal overreach and a  violation of both the letter and the  spirit of federal education law and the  US Constitution supporters of common  core like to portray these critics as  far-right extremists who are paranoid  about the government about a government  takeover but this is not true Diane  Ravich a respected historian of  education is hardly the darling of the  right especially recently on a February  26 of this year Ravitz wrote the  following piece titled why I suppose or  why I oppose Common Core standards for  comments below give a general overview  of many of the central concerns that  most critics have I mean a quota at  length quote I've been a long advocate  or I have long advocated for voluntary  national standards believing that it  would be helpful to States and to school  districts to have general guidelines  about what students should know  and be able to do as they progress  through school such standards I believe  should be volunteering not imposed by  the federal government for the past two  years I have steadfastly insisted that I  was neither for nor against Common Core  I was agnostic I wanted to see how they  worked in practice after much  deliberation I have come to the  conclusion that Common Core standards  effort is fundamentally flawed by the  process with which they have been  foisted upon the nation end quote  ravage goes on to explain her opposition  to common core quote the creation was  neither grassroots nor did it emanate  from the states in fact it was well  understood by the states that they would  not be eligible for a Race to the Top  funding unless they adopted Common Core  Standards federal law prohibits the US  Department of Education from prescribing  any curriculum but in this case the  Department figured out a clever way to  evade the letter of the law 46 states in  the District of Columbia signed on not  because Common Core Standards were  better than their own but because they  wanted their share of federal cash end  quote  the response from common core supporters  regarding the federal overreach has been  surprisingly weak michelle rhee are the  former Chancellor of the DC Public  Schools and a well known education a  reformer which I often times kind of  find myself agreeing with her is a  strong supporter of Common Core in a  speech just last week on Mackinac Island  in my home  State to political leaders and to  businesspeople  she said this quote the last majority of  states have adopted the standards I've  heard some rumblings from people who say  we don't like when the federal  government is telling us what to do we  don't like that you know what you should  not like the fact that China is kicking  our rear ends right now get over feeling  bad about the federal government and  feel bad that our kids are not competing  end quote  I certainly hope that our country's  commitment to the Constitution does not  simply hang on something as fragile as a  feeling that we are able to get over  Riis cavalier critique of those who are  concerned about federal overreach is  troubling but I for one appreciate her  honesty most supporters of common core  tried to hide behind words like  state-led and voluntary but anyone  willing to take an honest look at what  transcribed or what transpired between  2009 and 2011 would conclude that many  of these cash-strapped states already  under the burden of a budget shortfall  an expensive NCLB requirements were  seduced by a high pressured  time-sensitive sales pitch for I'm  adopting other standards that included  relief in the form of money and waivers  yes the states are ultimately  responsible for selling their  constitutional birthright for I'm a bowl  of porridge and given more time perhaps  many more states might have rejected a  poor bargain like this but perhaps it's  not too late initially Common Core  experienced widespread support even some  prominent Republicans were strong  supporters of it  like Jeb Bush of Florida chris Christie  of New Jersey and mitch daniels of  indiana but support for common core  seems to be weakening in some states  that originally had adopted other  standards are starting to take a second  look this spring the Michigan House of  Representatives voted essentially to  defund the implementation of common core  in Indiana the state Senate voted to  delay implementation of common core so  that the State Board of Education could  take a closer look at the quality cost  and the loss of local control connected  with the implementation of common core  in Indiana in April Indiana's new  governor Mike Pence agreed to take quote  a long hard look  end quote at common core and quickly  added that he was one of the few  politicians - initially opposed no the  no child left behind other states are  considering legislative action to delay  or defund Common Core within the last  nine months the following states have  held public forums or formal legislative  hearings to discuss delaying or  defunding common core South Carolina  North Carolina Georgia Florida Alabama  Louisiana Missouri Kansas Colorado Utah  Idaho South Dakota Ohio Pennsylvania and  Wisconsin in April the National  Republican Committee passed an anti  Common Core resolution saying that the  RNC quote rejects the common core plan  which creates and fits the country with  a straitjacket on academic freedom and  achievement end quote  never to be outdone the state of Texas  reiterated its opposition to Common Core  in early May the Texas House of  Representatives formally rejected the  standards by a margin of a hundred and  forty to two last month a poll of  Education insiders which include an ash  and state education leaders discovered  that support for common core is  beginning to fade the polls showed that  63% of those polled believe that the  states will implement some form of  moratorium on common core and it would  be wrong to assume that the opposition  to common core is coming only from the  right recently Randi Weingarten the  president of the second largest teachers  union in the United States with a  million members almost a million members  called for a moratorium on the use of  standardized tests that are connected  with Common Core miss wine Gardner  initially a strong supporter of Common  Core is concerned that aspects of Common  Core have been poorly implemented and  that without a quote mid-course  correction the entire effort will fall  apart the idea of a common nationwide  standard I'm sorry the idea of common  nationwide standards is appealing and as  I mentioned above the benefits of such  standard should not be ignored but  concerns over common core and especially  its implementation are real and  troubling any of these concerns cost  mediocrity federal overreach are serious  enough that states should consider  pausing and perhaps ultimately repealing  their adoption of the standards but a  much more fundamental concern exists  about common core that goes to the heart  of any educational experience recall the  comment from Arne Duncan from the  beginning of my talk he said we want  common career-ready standards the phrase  career ready or college and career-ready  appear throughout the common core  standards the opening page of the Common  Core document in  News 8 references to college and  career-readiness if any other goal is  mentioned such as literacy it is  subservient to the overarching goal the  catchphrase for common core printed  below its logo is preparing American  students for college and career common  core's mission statement reflects this  notion as well here's the entire mission  statement of Common Core quote the  Common Core Standards provide a  consistent clear understanding of what  students are expected to learn so  teachers and parents know what they need  to do to help them the standards are  designed to be robust and relevant to  the real world reflecting the knowledge  and skills that young people need here  it is for success in college and careers  and the final line I want you to pay  special attention to with American  students fully prepared for the future  our communities will be positioned and  here it is to compete successfully in a  global economy  end quote with such a mission it's easy  to see why many politicians and the  business leaders support Common Core  even critics of Common Core have adopted  the college and career-ready mantra and  now spend much of their time arguing how  Common Core will not prepare students  for the working world I understand this  I understand that this line of attack is  necessary if we have or if they have any  hope of stopping Common Core but what I  would like for us to consider here today  is whether or not career pressure career  preparation for a global economy should  be the ultimate education goal in  America in the 1920s in our 30s  progressive educators tried to devalue  impractical liberal arts education and  saw schools as mechanisms for preparing  students for particular roles within the  social structure during this era  schooling became job preparation but in  the ancient world job preparation was  known as a servile education because it  prepared students to serve a master in a  particular kind of work a modern  education Act theorists would say that  I'm being ridiculous to associate  ancient notion of servile education with  skills for the 21st century which will  allow students to adapt to an  ever-changing society but as long as  students are told that the end of  Education is a job or a career they will  forever be servants of some master joy  Pullman an education policy analyst for  the Heartland Institute and a Hillsdale  grad by the way recently won the Robert  Novak award to study and write about  common core Pullman is quickly becoming  one of the nation's experts on common  core at a recent hearing in Wisconsin on  common core standards Pullman addressed  common core's misguided focus in a  self-governing nation  I'm sorry quote in a self-governing  nation we need citizens who can govern  themselves the ability to support  oneself with meaningful work is an  important part but only a part of  self-government we needn't I'm sorry  when a nation expands workforce training  so that it crowds out other things that  rightly belong in education we end up  turning out neither good workers nor  good citizens and quote the ancients  knew that in order for men to be truly  free they must have a liberal education  that includes study of literature and  history mathematics and science music  and art yes man is made for work but  he's also made for  much more education should be about the  highest things we should study these  things but the stars plant cells square  roots Shakespeare's Hamlet Mozart's  Requiem Lincoln's Gettysburg Address not  simply because they'll get us into the  right college or into the right line of  work rather we should study these noble  things because they can tell us who we  are why we're here what our relationship  is to each other as human beings into  the world that surrounds us among other  things  commenting on common core standards  Anthony Esalen English professor at  Providence College said this what  appalls me most about the standards is  the cavalier contempt for great works of  human art and thought and literary form  it is sheer ignorance of the life of the  imagination we are not programming  machines we are teaching children we are  we are to be forming the minds and  hearts of men and women and we should  raise them to be human beings honouring  what is good and right and cherishing  what is beautiful if education has  become as common core openly declares  preparation for work in a global economy  then this situation is far worse than  Common Core critics ever anticipated and  the concerns about cost and quality and  yes even the constitutionality of Common  Core pale in comparison to the concerns  of the hearts minds and souls of  Americans children thank you very much

